From ae36fc10b5842bd24c84af4f6e61349e2df60747 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Travis Gilbert <1travisgilbert@gmail.com>
Date: Fri, 18 Sep 2026 14:51:43 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 2/2] feat(gpui-web): realize an editable leaf as a real
 browser control

A browser does not offer caret, selection, input-method composition,
autocorrect, spellcheck or the virtual keyboard as primitives. It offers
them only as properties of real elements, which is why reproducing them
over a painted canvas costs what it costs. The HTML-in-Canvas proposal
closes that gap, and this takes it where it exists.

The elements are drawn on a second transparent canvas stacked over the one
wgpu owns, not into wgpu's own output. The proposal's 2D call cannot get a
2D context on a canvas wgpu already holds, and its WebGL call needs the GL
texture name behind a `wgpu::Texture`, which wgpu exposes through no public
API; reaching it means transmuting `wgpu-hal` internals across a pinned
dependency. Two canvases and the browser's own compositor leave the
renderer untouched.

Support means all four entry points, because a partial implementation must
answer unsupported: an element that is laid out and never drawn is an
invisible control, which is worse than painting it. On every browser
shipping today the answer is unsupported, the layer is never built, and the
cost is four prototype lookups at window creation.

The accessibility mirror stands down for a leaf the browser realizes.
A real editable element already carries its own name, role and value to
assistive technology, so mirroring it would put the same control in the
tree twice and a screen reader would meet it twice.

A test may force either path through `window.__gpui_html_in_canvas`.
Forcing the native path on a browser that cannot draw the element is
reported and refused rather than honoured into an invisible control.
